<%# "Still going, or done?" #410.

    Sessions stayed open until somebody said otherwise, and workout 28 shows what that costs:
    **24h 46m**, because the first set was marked late one evening and the rest the next
    morning. Several sessions sit at `performed` with a handful of sets done and no ending at
    all, so nothing can tell "stopped here" from "still going".

    The finish control has existed since #218 and `finished_at` is already the honest end of a
    session. What was missing is anything that reaches for it on the lifter's behalf.

    **A question rather than an action**, which is what lets it fire on a guess. Twenty minutes
    of quiet is a lifter who has gone home, or one who is talking to somebody by the water
    fountain, and the app cannot tell those apart -- so it asks, and being wrong costs one tap.
    The automatic close, which is a write rather than a question, waits six hours.

    **"That's it" stamps the last completed set, not now.** #410 is explicit and it is the
    whole point: a prompt seen an hour later must not add that hour to how long the session
    took, which is the reading this issue exists to fix. The finish *button* at the top of the
    screen keeps stamping the current time and is right to -- tapping it is a lifter standing
    in the gym saying they are done, and the ten minutes spent putting plates away is time the
    session cost. Answering this is not that.

    Above the rest timer rather than replacing it, because the two can be true at once: a rest
    that ran to twenty minutes is exactly when this fires, and taking the countdown away at
    that moment would remove the one thing on screen that explains why it is asking.

    Hidden in markup rather than by a class, so the degradation is honest: with JavaScript off
    nothing here ever appears. That is right -- the question is only ever raised by a clock,
    and a scriptless page has none. Such a page loses nothing else: the finish control still
    works, and the automatic close is server-side and reaches it anyway. %>
<div id="session-nudge" hidden
     class="fixed inset-x-0 <%= nudge_offset %> z-40 border-t border-gray-300 bg-amber-50">
  <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-md items-center gap-3 px-4 py-3">
    <%# The number is the whole of the case for asking. "Still training?" on its own is a
        question out of nowhere; "nothing for 24 minutes" is the reason, and a lifter who has
        been chatting recognises it immediately. Announced rather than hidden: unlike the rest
        clock beside it this does not tick, so a screen reader says it once. %>
    <p class="min-w-0 flex-1 text-sm text-gray-900">
      Nothing logged for <span data-quiet-for class="font-semibold tabular-nums"></span>.
      Still training?
    </p>
    <%# Still going is a plain button and closes the bar. It re-arms: another quiet period
        later it asks again, because a lifter who says they are still here at twenty minutes
        may well have left at fifty, and asking once and then never again would make the
        first answer stand for the rest of the day. %>
    <button type="button" data-nudge-dismiss
            class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 shrink-0 border border-gray-300 bg-white px-3
                   text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 hover:bg-gray-50">
      Still going
    </button>
    <%# A real form, so this works the moment it is rendered and does not depend on the script
        for anything but being shown. `at=last` is what separates it from the finish control at
        the top of the screen: same route, different statement about when the session ended. %>
    <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/session/finish" method="post" class="shrink-0">
      <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/session/finish") %>
      <input type="hidden" name="at" value="last">
      <button type="submit"
              class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 bg-gray-900 px-3 text-sm font-medium text-white hover:bg-gray-800">
        That&rsquo;s it
      </button>
    </form>
  </div>
</div>

<%# The clock that decides when to ask.

    It counts from the cue's stamp against Date.now(), the same way the rest timer does and
    for the same reason: a tab the phone throttled in a pocket comes back with the right
    number rather than however many ticks it was allowed to run, and nothing here ever asks
    the browser what time it is on the server, so a phone with a wrong clock is still right.

    Ticking every five seconds rather than every 250ms, unlike the timer beside it. Nothing is
    waiting on a particular second here -- the difference between asking at 20:00 and 20:04 is
    nothing to anybody -- and this runs for the whole of a session rather than for the length
    of one rest.

    **Dismissing pushes the question forward rather than ending it.** `askedAt` is set to now,
    and the next question comes a full quiet period after that. A lifter who says they are
    still here at twenty minutes may have left at fifty.

    Re-reading the cue on every swap is what keeps it honest: a Done tap replaces the cue with
    a new stamp, the quiet restarts from there, and any dismissal is forgotten because the
    session plainly moved. %>
<script>
  (function () {
    var bar = document.getElementById('session-nudge');
    if (!bar) { return; }

    var quietFor = bar.querySelector('[data-quiet-for]');
    var lastAt = 0, quietAfter = 0, askedAt = 0;

    function spell(seconds) {
      var minutes = Math.floor(seconds / 60);
      return minutes < 60 ? minutes + 'm' : Math.floor(minutes / 60) + 'h ' + (minutes % 60) + 'm';
    }

    // Read off the cue rather than remembered, so a tap that moved the session moves this too.
    // A cue with no stamp is a session with nothing lifted, which has no quiet to measure.
    function readCue() {
      var cue = document.getElementById('quiet-cue');
      if (!cue) { return; }
      var at = parseFloat(cue.getAttribute('data-last-at'));
      var after = parseInt(cue.getAttribute('data-quiet-after'), 10);
      if (isNaN(at) || isNaN(after)) { lastAt = 0; return; }
      // A stamp this has not seen means the session moved, so the quiet restarts and any
      // earlier "still going" is spent -- it was an answer about a different silence.
      if (at !== lastAt) { askedAt = 0; bar.hidden = true; }
      lastAt = at * 1000;
      quietAfter = after;
    }

    function tick() {
      if (!lastAt) { return; }
      var since = (Date.now() - Math.max(lastAt, askedAt)) / 1000;
      if (since < quietAfter) { return; }
      quietFor.textContent = spell((Date.now() - lastAt) / 1000);
      bar.hidden = false;
    }

    bar.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
      if (!event.target.closest('[data-nudge-dismiss]')) { return; }
      askedAt = Date.now();
      bar.hidden = true;
    });

    // Re-read and re-decide together. A swap that moved the session should put the question
    // away at once rather than up to five seconds later, and a swap that did not should be
    // able to raise it at once too -- waiting for the next interval would make the bar appear
    // a beat after whatever the lifter just did, which reads as a reaction to the tap.
    function refresh() { readCue(); tick(); }

    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterSwap', refresh);
    setInterval(tick, 5000);
    refresh();
  })();
</script>
